---
layout: article
title: Usage monitors
description: Set thresholds on Appwrite usage metrics and monitor your whole project or an individual resource from the Console or a server SDK.
---

Usage monitors compare a metric with a threshold. In your project, open **Observe** > **Monitors** and click **Create monitor**. On Appwrite Cloud, choose **Usage** as the type. Under **Condition**, choose a **Metric**, what it **Applies to**, a comparator, and a threshold. **Applies to** only lists the scopes that metric supports.

# Choose a metric and scope {% #choose-a-metric-and-scope %}

Choose **The whole project** for a project-wide total, or a single resource when the metric supports it. Counters are summed over the period in **Counted over**. Gauges compare their latest reading.

| Metric | API name | Applies to | Compares |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Requests | `network.requests` | Project | Sum |
| Inbound bandwidth | `network.inbound` | Project | Sum |
| Outbound bandwidth | `network.outbound` | Project | Sum |
| Executions | `executions` | Project, function, or site | Sum |
| Execution GB-hours | `executions.mbSeconds` | Project, function, or site | Sum |
| Concurrent connections | `realtime.connections` | Project | Latest |
| Monthly active users | `users.mau` | Project | Latest |
| Sign-ups | `users` | Project | Rise over the period |
| OTP attempts | `auth.method.phone` | Project | Sum |
| Database reads | `databases.operations.reads` | Project or database | Sum |
| Database writes | `databases.operations.writes` | Project or database | Sum |
| Database storage | `databases.storage` | Project or database | Latest |
| Storage used | `storage` | Project, database, bucket, function, or site | Latest |
| File storage | `files.storage` | Project | Latest |
| Messages sent | `messages.sent` | Project | Sum |
| SMS messages sent | `messages.sms.sent` | Project | Sum |
| Messages failed | `messages.failed` | Project | Sum |
| Webhook events sent | `webhooks.events.sent` | Project | Sum |
| Webhook events failed | `webhooks.events.failed` | Project | Sum |
| Screenshots generated | `avatars.screenshotsGenerated` | Project | Sum |

On Appwrite Cloud, [dedicated databases](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/monitoring) add **CPU**, **Memory**, **Disk usage**, **Connections**, **Queries per second**, **Read IOPS**, and **Write IOPS**. Their API names are `dedicatedDatabases.cpu`, `dedicatedDatabases.memory`, `dedicatedDatabases.storage`, `dedicatedDatabases.connections`, `dedicatedDatabases.qps`, `dedicatedDatabases.iopsRead`, and `dedicatedDatabases.iopsWrite`, and each compares its latest reading.

Deleting a database, bucket, function, or site also deletes the monitors that watch it.

# Set the condition {% #set-the-condition %}

Under **Alert when the value is**, choose **greater than**, **greater than or equal**, **less than**, or **less than or equal**, and enter the threshold. A usage monitor compares one metric with one threshold.

**Counted over** sets the period a counter is summed over, from 5 minutes to 30 days. An outbound bandwidth monitor counted over 1 hour compares the sum over the preceding hour at each check. Gauges such as storage used compare their latest reading, so the Console hides **Counted over** for them.

{% only_dark %}
![Create monitor page with a usage condition of outbound bandwidth greater than 10 GB counted over 1 hour, and a chart of the last 24 hours showing it would not have fired](/images/docs/monitors/dark/usage-condition.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create monitor page with a usage condition of outbound bandwidth greater than 10 GB counted over 1 hour, and a chart of the last 24 hours showing it would not have fired](/images/docs/monitors/usage-condition.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Appwrite reads usage about 2 minutes behind real time, so requests still being recorded count toward a later check. If a check has no usage reading, the monitor keeps its status and any open alert. Check the monitor's last reading in the Console before adjusting the threshold.

# How often usage is checked {% #how-often-usage-is-checked %}

On Appwrite Cloud, usage monitors are checked every 15 minutes on the Free plan and every minute on paid plans. Free projects can have 3 usage monitors and Pro projects 25. See [Pricing](/pricing) for other plans.

On self-hosted Appwrite, each usage monitor is checked every 60 seconds. Set the `_APP_ALERTS_INTERVAL` environment variable to change the interval in seconds. Usage monitors read the usage stats that the [`_APP_USAGE_STATS`](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/configuration/environment-variables) variable turns on. It's enabled by default. If you set it to `disabled`, Appwrite never checks your usage monitors.

# Create with a server SDK {% #create-with-a-server-sdk %}

Create an [API key](/docs/partners/project/api-keys) with the `monitors.write` scope, then call `createUsageMonitor`. This monitor opens an alert when the project sends more than 1 GB of outbound bandwidth in an hour, and notifies owners and developers at **Error** severity.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const monitors = new sdk.Monitors(client);

const monitor = await monitors.createUsageMonitor({
    monitorId: 'outbound-bandwidth',
    name: 'Outbound bandwidth spike',
    rules: [sdk.Query.greaterThan('network.outbound', 1000000000)],
    window: 3600, // optional
    cooldown: 1800, // optional
    notify: ['owner', 'developer'], // optional
    severity: sdk.MonitorSeverity.Error // optional
});
```

```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.monitors import Monitors
from appwrite.query import Query
from appwrite.enums.monitor_severity import MonitorSeverity

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')

monitors = Monitors(client)

monitor = monitors.create_usage_monitor(
    monitor_id = 'outbound-bandwidth',
    name = 'Outbound bandwidth spike',
    rules = [Query.greater_than('network.outbound', 1000000000)],
    window = 3600, # optional
    cooldown = 1800, # optional
    notify = ['owner', 'developer'], # optional
    severity = MonitorSeverity.ERROR # optional
)
```

